  data:  # ae6f
    kind: work_item  # ae6f
    title: "scala: only 5-10% of external method calls carry an inferred receiver type at all -- var_types is fed by three syntactic shapes while Scala infers almost everything"  # ae6f
    status: todo_hard  # ae6f
    priority: 2  # ae6f
    parent:  # ae6f
    tags:  # ae6f
    - taint  # ae6f
    - io_boundary  # ae6f
    - analysis_quality  # ae6f
    - scala  # ae6f
    isbefore: []  # ae6f
    duplicate_of: []  # ae6f
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ae6f
    pr_ref:  # ae6f
    description: "FILED 2026-09-09 from WI-sigog's A/B (record:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/sigog_scala_slot_09092026/, PLAN.md pre-registered\nbefore any A-arm number existed).\n\nWI-sigog fixed the DISCARD: scala.py inferred the receiver's type and then\nhardcoded `external` into the module slot, so 0 of 40,970 external method-call\nedges were typed. Measuring the ceiling for that fix exposed a SECOND and much\nlarger defect sitting UPSTREAM of it, which that fix does not touch.\n\nMEASURED ON THE PRE-CHANGE (B) ARM, two independent repositories:\n\n    repo   scala external method-call edges   carrying receiver_type_hint\n    sbt                            10,985       1,106      10.1%\n    lila                           29,985       1,426       4.8%\n\n**About 90-95% of Scala external method calls carry NO inferred receiver type at\nall.** The module slot cannot be filled for them by any downstream change,\nbecause there is nothing to put in it. 10.1% / 4.8% is therefore the hard\nceiling on WI-sigog's fix, and on any method-kind catalogue matching in Scala.\n\nWHY var_types IS SO THIN. `_extract_edges_from_file` populates it from exactly\nthree shapes: function/method parameter annotations, `val x = new Foo()`, and\n`val x: Foo = f()` plus `class C(val svc: Service)`. Scala's type inference is\npervasive and idiomatic code writes almost none of these -- `val x = mk()`,\nfor-comprehension binders, lambda parameters, chained receivers\n(`a.b().c()`), and every `def` return type the caller never annotates are all\nuntyped here.\n\nONE CONFIRMED MECHANISM, cheap and separable: an INLINE-QUALIFIED annotation\nproduces no hint at all. `val b: java.io.File = mk(); b.createNewFile()` emits\n`meta` with NO `receiver_type_hint`, because the val branch calls\n`find_child_by_type(node, \"type_identifier\")` and a qualified type is not a\n`type_identifier` node. Verified on a 12-line probe 2026-09-09; the sibling\n`val a: File` on the line above DOES get a hint. This is a parse-shape miss, not\nan inference problem, and it is the first thing to fix here.\n\nTHE OTHER SHAPES ARE INFERENCE and are correspondingly harder: typing\n`val x = mk()` needs the return type of `mk`, which is the return-type registry\nobjc and swift already have (`_objc_return_type_registry` /\n`swift_return_type_registry`). That is the WI-dizag-A / WI-garar shape one\nlanguage over, and it is why this is filed as its own item rather than folded\ninto WI-sigog.\n\nDO NOT close this on WI-sigog's evidence. WI-sigog's closure is about the SLOT;\nthis item is about the HINT, and its own closure needs the hinted share to move\non corpus, measured the same way (the covariate scan is\n`sigog_scala_slot_09092026/` -- the `ceiling` computation in PLAN.md section 2)."  # ae6f
    fields: {}  # ae6f
- op: discuss  # 7f16
  at: '2026-09-10T11:46:58Z'  # 7f16
  by: agent  # 7f16
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7f16
  clock: 2  # 7f16
  nonce: 7f16  # 7f16
  message: "PROBE DONE — the item names ONE shape; there are at least THREE and they are one\nbug. Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/pokam_scala_09102026/PROBE_RESULT.md\n(probe.py, probe2.py, probe2_src/).\n\nThe filed \"inline-qualified annotation\" miss reproduces. It is not the family.\nDiscriminating pairs, each differing in exactly one thing, WITH the imports the\nmodule slot needs (my first pass omitted them, so even the working shape read\n`external` and the control proved nothing):\n\n  A1 annotation, imported bare name   val x: File            hint File    slot java.io.File\n  A2 annotation, inline-qualified     val x: java.io.File    hint None    slot external\n  B1 new, imported bare name          new File(\"p\")          hint File    slot java.io.File\n  B2 new, inline-qualified            new java.io.File(\"p\")  hint None    slot external\n  C1 annotation, GENERIC APPLIED      val x: Buffer[String]  hint None    slot external\n  C2 annotation, bare generic base    val x: Buffer          hint Buffer  slot scala.collection.mutable.Buffer\n  D1 parameter annotation, bare       def run(x: File)       hint File    slot java.io.File\n  E1 inferred from a call             val x = mk()           hint None    slot external\n\nTWO THINGS THE FILING DOES NOT CONTAIN:\n\n(1) **It is not the annotation path.** `new java.io.File(\"p\")` loses the hint too\n(B2) while `new File(\"p\")` keeps it (B1) — the same qualified-name blindness sits\non the `new` branch, which this item lists as one of the three shapes that WORK.\nA fix scoped to the val-annotation branch would leave B2 broken AND WOULD LOOK\nLIKE A FIX.\n\n(2) **A GENERIC APPLIED TYPE loses the hint (C1) while its bare base keeps it\n(C2).** `List[String]`, `Option[X]`, `Future[X]`, `Seq[X]`, `Map[K,V]` are\npervasive in idiomatic Scala and are plausibly a far LARGER population than\ninline-qualified names, which are rare in code that has an import block. This\nshape is not mentioned in the filing at all.\n\nMECHANISM: the hint is lost whenever the type node is anything other than a bare\n`type_identifier` — qualified or generic-applied, on either the annotation path\nor the `new` path. One structural fix at the type-node extraction, not three\nspecial cases.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and I am not going to let the probe stand in for it: a probe\nsays a shape MISSES, never how OFTEN it occurs. This item's acceptance metric is\nthe HINTED SHARE on sbt/lila (baseline 10.1% / 4.8%, sigog_scala_slot_09092026/\nPLAN.md §2), and that is what has to move. Which of the two shapes does the work\nis exactly the question a headline pools, so it gets pre-registered before the\nfix. And a moved hint is necessary, not sufficient (L5): the sigog record already\nwarns that String/Boolean/Thread are Scala's implicit imports, appear in no\nimport statement, and can never qualify to a module."  # 7f16
